    "html": "<p>Worth 10% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 10 points.</p><h3>Purpose</h3><p>Use this private journal to study three moments when a digital system shaped what you could see, choose, access, or do. Your entries should come from your own ordinary digital life. You do not need to disclose anything sensitive.</p><h3>What to complete</h3><p>Post three dated entries in this journal. Aim to complete Entry 1 by September 27, Entry 2 by October 4, and Entry 3 by October 18. You may return to an entry and improve it before the final due date.</p><h3>Include these six parts in every entry</h3><ol><li><strong>Original example:</strong> Add one example that you made or captured after removing personal details, such as a redacted screenshot, photo, short audio note, interface description, or exact message from the system.</li><li><strong>Your action:</strong> State exactly what you did.</li><li><strong>The system response:</strong> State exactly what the system showed, allowed, blocked, ranked, recommended, or requested.</li><li><strong>Course connection:</strong> Use one concept or assigned source from the relevant week. Explain the connection in your own words.</li><li><strong>Consequence and power:</strong> Explain who gained an advantage, who carried a burden or risk, and what part of the system produced that result.</li><li><strong>Reflection:</strong> Explain what you noticed now that you might not have noticed before.</li></ol><h3>Privacy and authorship</h3><ul><li>Remove names, account numbers, addresses, faces, and other identifying details.</li><li>You may describe a trace instead of uploading it.</li><li>Do not test a system with a made-up identity or ask another person to reveal private information.</li><li>Your grade is based on the specificity of your own trace, your course connections, and your reasoning. Generic examples do not meet the task.</li></ul><h3>How this journal is graded: 10 points</h3><ul><li>Specific examples and evidence: 3 points.</li><li>Accurate course concepts and sources: 2.5 points.</li><li>How the system creates consequences and distributes power: 3 points.</li><li>Reflection and growth: 1.5 points.</li></ul><h3>How to post your journal entries</h3><ul><li>Open this journal in Blackboard and find <strong>Entries</strong>. Start a separate entry for each required date or example.</li><li>Write directly in the journal editor. Put the entry number and date at the top. You do not need to upload a Word document.</li><li>If you choose an audio entry, attach the recording and a transcript, or add a Seneca-accessible recording link.</li><li>Select <strong>Post</strong>, then reopen the entry to confirm it is there. You may edit your entries before the due date. Only you and the teaching team can read this journal.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p>Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Release and due dates</h3><p><strong>Released:</strong> September 21, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p><strong>Due:</strong> October 18, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p>",
