    "name": "Digital Systems Field Notes Portfolio 2",
    "summary": "Continue the private field-notes practice with three new moments from your own digital life. This phase asks you to notice how a system sorts, predicts, rewards, restricts, or defines a person or choice.",
    "html": "<p>Worth 10% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 10 points.</p><h3>Purpose</h3><p>Continue the private field-notes practice with three new moments from your own digital life. This phase asks you to notice how a system sorts, predicts, rewards, restricts, or defines a person or choice.</p><h3>What to complete</h3><p>Post three dated entries in this journal. Aim to complete Entry 4 by November 8, Entry 5 by November 15, and Entry 6 by November 29. Each entry must use a different digital moment. You may improve an entry before the final due date.</p><h3>Include these six parts in every entry</h3><ol><li><strong>Original example:</strong> Add one example you made or captured after removing personal details, or describe it precisely.</li><li><strong>Your action:</strong> State exactly what you did.</li><li><strong>The system response:</strong> State what the system showed, allowed, blocked, ranked, recommended, predicted, or requested.</li><li><strong>Course connection:</strong> Use one concept or assigned source from the relevant week. Explain the connection in your own words.</li><li><strong>Consequence and power:</strong> Identify who gained an advantage, who carried a burden or risk, and what design choice produced the result.</li><li><strong>Reflection:</strong> Explain how your interpretation has changed since Portfolio 1.</li></ol><h3>Privacy and authorship</h3><ul><li>Remove names, account numbers, addresses, faces, and other identifying details.</li><li>You may describe a trace instead of uploading it.</li><li>Do not test a system with a made-up identity or ask another person to reveal private information.</li><li>Specific personal examples and accurate course connections matter more than polished language.</li></ul><h3>How this journal is graded: 10 points</h3><ul><li>Specific examples and evidence: 3 points.</li><li>Accurate course concepts and sources: 2.5 points.</li><li>How the system creates consequences and distributes power: 3 points.</li><li>Reflection and growth: 1.5 points.</li></ul><h3>How to post your journal entries</h3><ul><li>Open this journal in Blackboard and find <strong>Entries</strong>. Start a separate entry for each required date or example.</li><li>Write directly in the journal editor. Put the entry number and date at the top. You do not need to upload a Word document.</li><li>If you choose an audio entry, attach the recording and a transcript, or add a Seneca-accessible recording link.</li><li>Select <strong>Post</strong>, then reopen the entry to confirm it is there. You may edit your entries before the due date. Only you and the teaching team can read this journal.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p>Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Release and due dates</h3><p><strong>Released:</strong> November 2, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p><strong>Due:</strong> November 29, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p>",
