    "id": "cartography",
    "title": "6. Personal Cartography (15%)",
    "name": "Personal Cartography",
    "summary": "Create a personal map of how digital systems have shaped your choices, access, visibility, or sense of what is normal. The map must be rooted in your own course evidence, but it does not require private or painful disclosure.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 15% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 15 points.</p><h3>Your task</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Create a personal map of how digital systems have shaped your choices, access, visibility, or sense of what is normal. The map must be rooted in your own course evidence, but it does not require private or painful disclosure.</p><h3>Build five map points</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Your five points should create a route, not a list. You may adapt these functions to fit your story:</p><ol style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Starting place:</strong> Where does a digital system enter an ordinary part of your life?</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Gate or threshold:</strong> Where does a system rank, filter, permit, deny, or redirect?</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Visibility or translation:</strong> Where are you made too visible, not visible enough, or translated into a category?</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Response or repair:</strong> Where did you adapt, question, resist, or imagine a different design?</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Route forward:</strong> What would increased agency or a more just system look like now?</li></ol><h3>Evidence your map must use</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Use one specific example from each of your six Field Notes. You may place more than one Field Note at a map point.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Use at least two other course pieces, such as an assigned source, a weekly scenario, the Canadian Technology Investigation, or Design the Repair.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Include a then-and-now pair that shows how your interpretation of one system, concept, or experience changed across the course.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">At every map point, connect a personal example to a course idea. Explain the system or power relationship and what you now understand or would do.</li></ul><h3>Choose a form that fits your route</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">You may create a visual map, annotated photo sequence, zine, slide story, audio map, or another accessible form. Submit the map plus either a three to four minute narrated walkthrough or a 600 to 900 word guided narrative.</p><h3>Privacy and accessibility</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Use approximate locations, composite scenes, redaction, or description when exact details would identify you or someone else.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Do not include account information, addresses, faces, private messages, or another person’s story without permission.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Provide alt text, a transcript, captions, or an equivalent format where your chosen medium needs it.</li></ul><h3>Show your process</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Include two dated draft snapshots or planning notes and a short source list. Briefly explain one major choice you changed while building the map. Include this process record with your final submission.</p><h3>How it will be graded: 15 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Personal anchors and specificity: 4 points</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Course synthesis: 4 points</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Map argument and change over time: 4 points</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Creative communication and accessibility: 2 points</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Process, privacy, and source trace: 1 point</li></ul><h3>How to submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assignment and select <strong>Start attempt</strong>. Write or paste your written work into the <strong>Submission</strong> editor using the headings in these instructions. A Word upload is optional.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Insert your required visual into the editor with a text description, or attach it as an accessible file. If you choose an audio or video format, attach the recording and captions or a transcript. A working Seneca OneDrive or Microsoft Stream link is also acceptable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Keep all required parts together in this assignment. Before submitting, check that your text, attachments, and links open correctly.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select <strong>Submit</strong>, wait for Blackboard’s confirmation, and keep the receipt. If you save a draft instead, return to submit it before the deadline.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Release and due dates</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Released:</strong> November 30, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due:</strong> December 11, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p>",
